WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.48
This is journey eight of Hewn, the software factory: stop and restart all workers.

00:00:06.48 --> 00:00:11.53
Sometimes everything needs to pause at once: a bad model release, a spending scare,

00:00:11.53 --> 00:00:13.78
or maintenance on the build machines.

00:00:13.78 --> 00:00:17.30
An Owner needs one switch that halts every worker quickly,

00:00:17.30 --> 00:00:20.95
keeps every project's progress, and holds until they say go.

00:00:20.95 --> 00:00:25.94
This video walks through how it was built: the flow, the leasing switch, the halt,

00:00:25.94 --> 00:00:28.12
the worker, the page, and the tests.

00:00:29.27 --> 00:00:32.45
An Owner presses Stop all workers and confirms.

00:00:32.45 --> 00:00:37.57
Every worker halts its active run within ten seconds and leases no new work.

00:00:37.57 --> 00:00:42.90
A banner shows that workers are stopped, until the Owner presses Start workers,

00:00:42.90 --> 00:00:46.82
and each halted run then resumes from its last checkpoint.

00:00:46.82 --> 00:00:52.08
Viewers see the banner, never the controls, and the API answers four oh three.

00:00:53.23 --> 00:00:54.70
Here is the whole flow.

00:00:54.70 --> 00:00:59.82
Each worker holds its control request open while it works, as it did for Cancel.

00:00:59.82 --> 00:01:04.74
Stop all workers saves the leasing switch first, then halts every leased run,

00:01:04.74 --> 00:01:05.95
project by project.

00:01:05.95 --> 00:01:10.69
Each held request answers Halt, and the worker stops the step in progress.

00:01:10.69 --> 00:01:15.23
Start workers queues the halted runs again, then turns leasing back on,

00:01:15.23 --> 00:01:18.49
and the next lease carries on after the checkpoint.

00:01:19.64 --> 00:01:24.65
The switch is one row in the database, so a restarted server stays stopped.

00:01:24.65 --> 00:01:30.19
The lease is granted in a single update, and that update now checks the switch too.

00:01:30.19 --> 00:01:33.87
Once Stop has saved it, no worker can be granted a run,

00:01:33.87 --> 00:01:37.47
and a run granted a moment before is found and halted.

00:01:38.62 --> 00:01:43.52
The stop command saves the switch, then halts each leased run under its project's

00:01:43.52 --> 00:01:43.82
lock.

00:01:43.82 --> 00:01:47.45
A halted run keeps its worker, its epoch and its checkpoint,

00:01:47.45 --> 00:01:52.10
but it no longer fences anything, so every write the worker tries is refused.

00:01:52.10 --> 00:01:56.82
The run log says who stopped the workers, and which step the run resumes from.

00:01:56.82 --> 00:02:01.59
Then the command wakes every held control request and waits, up to ten seconds,

00:02:01.59 --> 00:02:03.05
for each worker to hear.

00:02:03.05 --> 00:02:05.89
Any worker that doesn't is named in the answer.

00:02:06.24 --> 00:02:11.07
On the API side, the control poll now answers Halt for a halted run,

00:02:11.07 --> 00:02:14.13
and records Run halted at step, in the log.

00:02:15.28 --> 00:02:20.54
In the worker, a Halt answer aborts the step in progress, exactly like Cancel.

00:02:20.54 --> 00:02:25.06
After Start workers, the same run is leased again with a new epoch,

00:02:25.06 --> 00:02:29.17
and the worker carries on from the step after its checkpoint.

00:02:30.32 --> 00:02:33.92
Owners get Stop all workers at the foot of the sidebar.

00:02:33.92 --> 00:02:38.82
It opens a confirmation listing every online worker and what it is running.

00:02:38.82 --> 00:02:42.95
While Hewn waits, the button counts the workers it is stopping.

00:02:43.30 --> 00:02:48.26
Once they have stopped, a banner on every page says who stopped them and when,

00:02:48.26 --> 00:02:50.61
and the sidebar offers Start workers.

00:02:50.61 --> 00:02:54.75
The new Workers page shows each worker holding at its checkpoint.

00:02:54.75 --> 00:02:59.14
If a worker doesn't answer, the dialog names it and offers Try again.

00:02:59.14 --> 00:03:04.48
It works in dark mode and on a phone, and a Viewer sees the banner with no controls.

00:03:05.63 --> 00:03:08.24
Each slice began with its tests failing.

00:03:08.24 --> 00:03:13.51
The backend gained five tests against the real API: stopping two running projects

00:03:13.51 --> 00:03:17.61
within ten seconds, starting them again from their checkpoints,

00:03:17.61 --> 00:03:22.10
the switch surviving a server restart, a worker that does not answer,

00:03:22.10 --> 00:03:23.08
and the Viewer.

00:03:23.08 --> 00:03:28.16
The worker gained a test that halts a run and resumes it after its checkpoint.

00:03:28.51 --> 00:03:31.85
All green, the backend has one hundred and ninety tests,

00:03:31.85 --> 00:03:36.56
the worker one hundred and thirty nine, and the frontend eighty one unit tests.

00:03:36.56 --> 00:03:39.66
End to end, two new pipeline tests use real workers.

00:03:39.66 --> 00:03:43.90
One stops two running projects, checks the banner and the Workers page,

00:03:43.90 --> 00:03:48.37
and starts them again, checking that each run resumes at the step after its

00:03:48.37 --> 00:03:49.03
checkpoint.

00:03:49.03 --> 00:03:52.79
The other stops a silent worker, and checks what a Viewer sees.

00:03:52.79 --> 00:03:55.41
There are fifty two end to end tests in all.

00:03:56.56 --> 00:04:01.74
Here are two projects, Ledgerline and Tidewater, each leased by its own worker.

00:04:01.74 --> 00:04:06.92
The Owner presses Stop all workers, and the dialog lists both workers and their

00:04:06.92 --> 00:04:07.51
projects.

00:04:07.51 --> 00:04:08.63
Stop all workers.

00:04:08.63 --> 00:04:11.84
Stopping two workers, while each worker hears it.

00:04:11.84 --> 00:04:16.76
Then the banner appears, and both workers are holding at their checkpoints.

00:04:17.11 --> 00:04:21.80
On Ledgerline's page, the log shows the halt, and the step it resumes from,

00:04:21.80 --> 00:04:23.17
under the same banner.

00:04:23.52 --> 00:04:25.77
Now the Owner presses Start workers.

00:04:25.77 --> 00:04:30.65
The banner goes, the run is queued again, and it carries on where it left off.

00:04:31.00 --> 00:04:35.79
And when the workers have gone silent, the dialog says they didn't confirm,

00:04:35.79 --> 00:04:37.89
names them, and offers Try again.

00:04:37.89 --> 00:04:41.09
A Viewer sees the banner, with no controls at all.

00:04:42.24 --> 00:04:44.01
Journey eight is complete.

00:04:44.01 --> 00:04:48.04
Stop all workers halts every active run within ten seconds,

00:04:48.04 --> 00:04:51.51
over the control request each worker already holds.

00:04:51.51 --> 00:04:56.36
The leasing switch is stored, so nothing is leased until Start workers,

00:04:56.36 --> 00:04:57.79
even after a restart.

00:04:57.79 --> 00:05:01.13
And every halted run resumes from its checkpoint.

00:05:01.13 --> 00:05:04.40
Next comes journey nine: recover a failed stage.
